    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best Christmas gifts for men under $50 are not cheap versions of expensive things. They are the best versions of everyday things: the skillet, the pocket knife, the headlamp and the socks a man will reach for every week for years. Spend your budget there, and nobody will think about what it cost.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked Christmas Gifts for Men Under $50
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most lists of cheap Christmas gifts for men are written backwards. They start with a price ceiling and fill it with whatever is trending, which is how you end up with a beer-can holster and a novelty mug that lives at the back of a cupboard by February. We started from the other end: what does a man actually reach for in a normal week, and which of those things can be bought in a version that is the best of its kind for less than $50?
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pick on this page is a current model from a brand with a long track record, and every one had a list price under $50 when we checked the maker's own store or a major retailer in October 2026. We did not include anything we could not identify down to the exact model, because a vague product name is how buyers end up with a knockoff. Holiday pricing moves every week, so we sort by price band rather than printing exact figures that will be wrong by December.
          </p>

          <p>
            We put each candidate through three questions. Will he use it in the first week? Will he still be using it next Christmas? If it broke, would he buy the same thing again? A surprising number of popular gifts fail the second question. The ones that pass tend to be plain, well-made objects that do one job very well: a skillet, a pocket knife, a headlamp, a pair of socks with a lifetime guarantee.
          </p>

          <p>
            This site covers tools, home improvement, outdoor gear and grooming, so the list leans that way. Three picks also appear in our own buying guides, and where they do we say so and link to the full comparison. If your budget stretches further, our main <a href="/best-christmas-gifts-for-men/">Christmas gifts for men</a> guide covers the bigger-ticket ideas, and if he already owns the obvious things, our guide to <a href="/christmas-gifts-for-men-who-have-everything/">Christmas gifts for men who have everything</a> is built for exactly that problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="not-cheap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How to Make a Cheap Gift Not Feel Cheap
          </h2>

          <p>
            Price is not what makes a gift feel cheap. Mismatch is. A bar of soap from someone who knows he showers after splitting wood reads as thoughtful; a gadget that clearly came from the impulse rack by the register reads as an afterthought, even when it cost twice as much. The goal with affordable Christmas gifts for men is to show that you paid attention, not that you spent.
          </p>

          <p>
            The most reliable rule is to buy the best version of a small thing rather than the worst version of a big thing. Forty dollars buys a forgettable Bluetooth speaker or a forgettable drill, but it also buys the best keychain multi-tool, the classic Stanley bottle or a cast iron skillet that will outlive the house it is used in. When the gift is the category leader, nobody thinks about what it cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pairing helps more than wrapping paper ever will. Two or three small, related items in one box look like a kit someone put together on purpose. A Hario V60 dripper with a pack of filters and a bag of beans from a local roaster is a morning ritual. A pocket knife plus a headlamp is an everyday carry kit. A thermometer tucked into a skillet is steak night. Each item is inexpensive, but the box tells a story.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then write the note. One sentence about why you picked it, such as noticing his flashlight is always dead or that his knuckles crack every winter, does more than any amount of ribbon. Include a gift receipt for anything that has to fit, and leave out any mention of a sale. Small Christmas gifts for men land best when they look like you were thinking about him in November, not panicking on the twenty-third.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, skip anything labelled as a gift set for him in a plastic tray. Those sets are designed to look generous on a shelf and are usually built around the cheapest version of each item. One good thing beats five mediocre ones every time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The 20 Best Christmas Gifts for Men Under $50
          </h2>

          <p>
            The picks are ordered by price band: our overall pick first, then stocking stuffers under $20, gifts under $25, and the best of what $50 buys. Every product is a current model with a list price under $50 on the maker's or a major retailer's site when we checked in October 2026. Specifications come from the maker's listing, and where owners commonly report a strength or a weakness we say so. Three picks also appear in our own buying guides, which we name in their descriptions and link in the sections below. Prices shift during the holidays, so we use price bands rather than exact figures.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
                      {index + 1}
                    </span>
                    {productImages[product.id] && (
                      <img
                        src={productImages[product.id]}
                        alt={product.name}
                        loading="lazy"
                        width="40"
                        height="40"
                        class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-white object-contain p-1 shrink-0"
                      />
                    )}
                    <div>
                      <h3 class="text-white font-black text-lg leading-snug m-0"><span class="block text-[#38BDF8] text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide mb-1 sm:hidden">{product.badge}</span>{product.name}</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs">{product.category}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                  <span class="bg-[#38BDF8]/20 text-[#38BDF8] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold hidden sm:block">
                    {product.badge}
                  </span>
                </div>
                <div class="p-6">
                  {product.rating && product.reviewCount && (
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4">
                    <div class="flex text-yellow-400">
                      {[...Array(5)].map((_, i) => (
                        <svg
                          class={`w-4 h-4 ${i < Math.floor(product.rating) ? "fill-current" : "text-gray-200"}`}
                          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
                          viewBox="0 0 20 20"
                          fill="currentColor"
                        >
                          <path d="M9.049 2.927c.3-.921 1.603-.921 1.902 0l1.07 3.292a1 1 0 00.95.69h3.462c.969 0 1.371 1.24.588 1.81l-2.8 2.034a1 1 0 00-.364 1.118l1.07 3.292c.3.921-.755 1.688-1.54 1.118l-2.8-2.034a1 1 0 00-1.175 0l-2.8 2.034c-.784.57-1.838-.197-1.539-1.118l1.07-3.292a1 1 0 00-.364-1.118L2.98 8.72c-.783-.57-.38-1.81.588-1.81h3.461a1 1 0 00.951-.69l1.07-3.292z" />
                        </svg>
                      ))}
                    </div>
                    <span class="text-gray-500 text-sm font-bold">{product.rating}</span>
                    <span class="text-gray-400 text-xs">({product.reviewCount.toLocaleString()} reviews)</span>
                  </div>
                  )}

                  <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">{product.description}</p>

                  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-6">
                    <div class="bg-green-50 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-green-700 mb-3">Pros</p>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.pros.map((pro) => (
                          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-green-800">
                            <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-green-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg>
                            {pro}
                          </li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 rounded-xl p-4">
                      <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-3">Cons</p>
                      <ul class="space-y-2">
                        {product.cons.map((con) => (
                          <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-red-800">
                            <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-red-500 mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M6 18L18 6M6 6l12 12"></path></svg>
                            {con}
                          </li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-gray-100">
                    <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-1">The Bottom Line</p>
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                  </div>

                  <a
                    href={amazonLink(product.amazonUrl, product.name, product.id)}
                    target="_blank"
                    rel="sponsored nofollow noopener"
                    class="mt-6 block bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white text-center py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm"
                  >
                    Check Price &rarr;
                  </a>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="by-price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is Worth the Money at Each Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            Each price band buys a different kind of gift, and knowing the difference keeps you from overspending on the wrong thing or underspending on the right one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Christmas gifts for men under $20</h3>

          <p>
            Under $20, the winners are consumables and single-purpose tools. Soap, hand cream, a meat thermometer, a chainsaw file kit and a magnetic stud finder all do one job well at this price, because none of them need expensive materials or electronics to work. This is where stocking stuffers for men live. What does not work under $20 is anything with a battery and a brand you have never heard of: cheap earbuds, mystery chargers and no-name smart gadgets are where the corners get cut.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Christmas gifts for men under $25</h3>

          <p>
            The extra five dollars opens up a surprising amount. Under $25 you can buy genuine name-brand everyday carry, including the Victorinox Classic SD, the Milwaukee Fastback utility knife and Black Diamond's Astro 300 headlamp. These are the same products enthusiasts carry, not budget imitations of them. If you are picking one inexpensive Christmas gift for a man you do not know well, this is the price where the odds are best.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Christmas gifts for men under $50</h3>

          <p>
            Under $50 is where the best-in-class gifts appear: a Lodge skillet, an AeroPress, a Gerber Dime, a OneBlade, a Stanley bottle and Darn Tough socks. At this price you can buy something he would describe as the one to get. It is also the band where you need to be careful, because $50 buys a lot of mediocre electronics too. The rule that holds: favor simple, durable things from the companies that define their category, and check ecosystem fit before buying tech.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price band</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best buys</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to avoid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under $20</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Consumables, single-job tools, grill thermometer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No-name electronics and chargers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under $25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Name-brand pocket knife, utility knife, headlamp</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bargain multi-piece tool kits</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under $50</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cast iron, coffee makers, multi-tools, socks, trimmers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid-range gadgets that do a little of everything</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="by-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Gifts by Type: Match the Gift to the Man
          </h2>

          <p>
            The fastest way to choose is to start with how he spends a Saturday, then pick the best affordable item from that world.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">If he is into</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Start with</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Also consider</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tools and the garage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Klein 32500 11-in-1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Milwaukee Fastback, CH Hanson stud finder, Mechanix gloves</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Kitchen and coffee</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lodge 12-inch skillet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">AeroPress, Hario V60, ThermoPro TP19H</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Grooming</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Philips OneBlade 360 Face</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">O'Keeffe's Working Hands, Duke Cannon soap</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Outdoors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Black Diamond Astro 300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Darn Tough socks, Stanley bottle, Oregon file kit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Everyday carry</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Victorinox Classic SD</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Gerber Dime, Casio A158WA-1</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Desk and tech</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Anker Nano power bank</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Apple AirTag for iPhone users</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For the garage guy, buy tools that fill a gap rather than duplicate what he has. Most men already own a hammer and a tape measure. Fewer own a good multi-bit driver, a utility knife they like, or work gloves that fit. If you want a full tool set, good ones cost more than $50; our <a href="/best-tekton-hand-tools/">Tekton hand tools guide</a> covers the sets that are worth stretching for.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the cook, cast iron and an instant-read thermometer are the two cheapest upgrades that change results, and the Lodge is also the budget pick in our <a href="/best-induction-cookware/">induction cookware guide</a>. For the coffee drinker, the AeroPress is the safer gift because it forgives sloppy technique; the V60 suits someone who already weighs his beans. Grooming is the riskiest category because men are particular, so stick to unscented or classic products and to tools that fix a problem he has mentioned, such as cracked hands. Our <a href="/best-mens-back-shavers/">back shaver guide</a> covers a gift category most men will never ask for but often appreciate.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the outdoorsman, light, warmth and hot coffee are always welcome, and if he runs a saw, the Oregon file kit is the budget pick in our <a href="/best-chainsaw-sharpener/">chainsaw sharpener guide</a>. For the everyday carry crowd, smaller is better: the knife or tool that rides on a keychain gets used far more than the one that is too big to carry. For tech, check his phone before buying anything, because ecosystem mistakes are the most common reason cheap tech gifts end up unused.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stocking-stuffers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Stocking Stuffers for Men That Actually Get Used
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stocking stuffers for men have a reputation problem. The category is full of novelty socks, joke gadgets and candy, most of which is forgotten by lunch on Christmas Day. The better approach is to treat the stocking as a place for small, useful upgrades to things he already does: wash, cook, fix, carry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consumables are the safest bet because they never become clutter. Duke Cannon's oversized bar and O'Keeffe's Working Hands are both inexpensive, both commonly recommended by men who work with their hands, and both run out, which means a good pick becomes a repeat gift. If he likes the scent you chose, you have solved next year's stocking too.
          </p>

          <p>
            The next tier is tiny tools with one clear job. A magnetic stud finder like the CH Hanson, our magnetic pick in the <a href="/best-stud-finder/">stud finder guide</a>, a chainsaw file kit or a meat thermometer is the kind of thing a man will not buy for himself because it never feels urgent until the day he needs it. That is exactly what makes them good small gifts for men at Christmas: they arrive before the problem does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size matters for a stocking in a literal sense. The Victorinox Classic SD, the Gerber Dime, the Casio watch and an AirTag all fit in a stocking toe, which lets a modest gift feel like a discovery at the bottom. Larger items like the Lodge skillet belong under the tree. If you want christmas gifts for men under $20 that still feel substantial, combine two from this list rather than buying one bigger novelty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What to Skip: Gimmicks That Break by New Year's
          </h2>

          <p>
            The cheap end of the gift market is where the most money gets wasted, because the items that photograph well are rarely the ones that last. A few categories come up again and again in owner complaints and returns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Credit-card multi-tools and novelty survival cards look clever in the package and do almost nothing in the hand. The edges are too thin to grip and the tools too small to apply any force. A real keychain tool like the Gerber Dime costs a little more and actually works. The same logic applies to no-name magnetic wristbands and bargain tool kits with a hundred pieces in a plastic case: the sockets and bits in those kits are often soft enough to round over on the first stubborn bolt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary of cheap electronics from brands you cannot find a website for. Unbranded earbuds, phone chargers and power banks are where safety and longevity corners get cut, and a dead gadget in March is worse than no gift. If you want tech under $50, buy from a company like Anker or Apple that stands behind its product and lists real specifications.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whiskey stones are another common miss. They are a popular gift, but they chill a drink less than ice does, because ice absorbs a large amount of heat as it melts and stone does not. Grooming kits from unfamiliar brands, tactical flashlights with impossible lumen claims and anything sold as a gift set for him are best left on the shelf. The pattern is the same each time: if a product's main selling point is how it looks in the box, it probably will not survive January.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="last-minute" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Last-Minute Christmas Gifts for Men
          </h2>

          <p>
            If it is December 22 and nothing is wrapped, the good news is that many of the picks on this page are mainstream products carried by big-box stores rather than niche online brands. Last minute Christmas gifts for men do not have to come from a gas-station display.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lodge cast iron is widely stocked at department stores, hardware stores and outdoor retailers. Milwaukee and Klein hand tools are standard stock at the big home improvement chains, and Mechanix gloves are common there too. Apple AirTags and Anker chargers are usually on the shelf at electronics and big-box stores. O'Keeffe's hand cream and Duke Cannon soap are both sold through mass retailers. Darn Tough socks and Black Diamond headlamps are typically carried by outdoor shops. Check local stock online before driving, because holiday weeks empty shelves fast.
          </p>

          <p>
            For online orders, ignore the generic shipping cutoff advice on gift sites and look at the delivery date shown at checkout for your own address. If it says after Christmas, buy something you can pick up in a store, or print a photo of what is coming and put that in the card. A note saying a Stanley bottle is on the way still beats a panic buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The worst last-minute habit is buying from the impulse rack because it is right there. A Victorinox Classic SD or a ThermoPro thermometer bought on the way home is a far better gift than a novelty item from the same store, and it costs about the same.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bundles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Three Gift Bundles That Stay Under $50
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bundling two or three of the smaller picks is the easiest way to give something that looks generous on a tight budget. At typical list prices, each of these combinations comes in under $50, though holiday sales may make them cheaper.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The everyday carry kit</h3>

          <p>
            A Victorinox Classic SD and a Black Diamond Astro 300 headlamp together cover the two small problems most men face every week: something that needs cutting and somewhere that needs light. Both are name-brand products enthusiasts actually carry, and the pair fits in a small box with room for a note.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The workbench stocking</h3>

          <p>
            A CH Hanson magnetic stud finder, a jar of O'Keeffe's Working Hands and a Duke Cannon brick of soap make a stocking for the man who spends weekends on projects: something to find the stud, something for his hands afterward and something to wash up with. All three are inexpensive, and none of them duplicate the tools he probably already owns.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The morning coffee kit</h3>

          <p>
            A Hario V60 plastic dripper, a pack of V60 filters and a bag of beans from a local roaster turn a cheap cone into a complete morning ritual. If he already owns a burr grinder, this is a real upgrade; if he does not, ask the roaster to grind the bag for pour-over.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the gift set from the endcap</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pre-packaged gift sets for men are built to look generous on a shelf, not to work well. They usually pair the cheapest version of several items in a plastic tray. One category-leading product, like a Victorinox knife or a Lodge skillet, will be used for years, while the tray set is usually in a drawer by spring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a cheap version of an expensive category</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Under $50, a mid-range drill, speaker or earbuds will be the worst version of something he could buy much better. Spend the same money on the best version of a smaller thing instead. A great headlamp beats a bad flashlight, and a great multi-bit driver beats a bad power tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring his phone and ecosystem</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An AirTag is close to useless for an Android user, and a charger with the wrong connector goes straight back in the box. Before buying tech, check what phone he carries and what cables he uses. This takes ten seconds and avoids the most common way cheap tech gifts go unused.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Guessing on sizes for things that need to fit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Work gloves and socks are good gifts only if they fit. Mechanix and Darn Tough both publish sizing charts, and his shoe size is easy to find in his closet. If you cannot check, include a gift receipt and mention that swapping sizes is expected rather than a sign the gift missed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting refills and accessories</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some gifts need a second purchase to work well. The OneBlade needs replacement blades over time, the V60 needs paper filters, and an AirTag needs a holder to attach to keys. Add the small extra to the box so he can use the gift on Christmas morning instead of waiting for a store run.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are good Christmas gifts for men under $50?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best Christmas gifts for men under $50 are well-made, single-purpose items from category-leading brands: a Lodge 12-inch cast iron skillet, an AeroPress coffee maker, a Gerber Dime multi-tool, a Philips OneBlade, a Stanley vacuum bottle or Darn Tough socks. Each is something he would describe as the one to get, rather than a budget version of something better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good Christmas gift for a man under $25?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Under $25, name-brand everyday carry is the sweet spot. The Victorinox Swiss Army Classic SD, the Milwaukee Fastback utility knife and the Black Diamond Astro 300 headlamp are all products enthusiasts actually carry, and they are used weekly. If you are buying for a man you do not know well, a small pocket knife or headlamp is a very safe choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are good stocking stuffers for men?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best stocking stuffers for men are consumables and small, single-job tools. Duke Cannon soap, O'Keeffe's Working Hands hand cream, a ThermoPro instant-read thermometer, a magnetic stud finder and a chainsaw file kit all work well, and most come in under $20. They are useful from day one and either get used up or fix a problem he has been putting off.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it rude to give an inexpensive Christmas gift?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. A cheap gift feels cheap only when it looks random. An inexpensive Christmas gift for a man that matches how he actually spends his time, comes with a one-line note about why you chose it and is the best version of its kind reads as thoughtful. Plenty of men prefer a $20 tool they use every week to an expensive gadget they never touch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What can I buy for a man at the last minute?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many picks here are carried by mainstream stores. Lodge cast iron, Milwaukee and Klein hand tools, Mechanix gloves, Apple AirTags, Anker chargers, O'Keeffe's and Duke Cannon are widely stocked at big-box, hardware and electronics stores. Check local inventory online before driving. If something will not arrive in time, print a photo of it and put that in the card.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What cheap gifts for men should I avoid?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Skip credit-card multi-tools, no-name electronics, hundred-piece bargain tool kits, gift sets in plastic trays and whiskey stones. These items tend to look good in the package and disappoint in use. The common thread is that their main selling point is presentation. Spending the same money on one well-made item from a known brand is almost always the better gift.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do I get a man who already has everything?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                When he already owns the obvious things, focus on upgrades to items he uses daily, like better socks, a better utility knife or a better coffee maker, or on consumables he will not buy himself. For more ideas built for exactly this situation, see our guide to Christmas gifts for men who have everything, which goes beyond the under-$50 budget.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

